<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afon Mawddach</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as River Mawddach</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mawddach is a river of Gwynedd in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> that flows from the hills north of Dolgellau into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardigan-bay">Cardigan Bay</a> at Barmouth through a wide, wooded estuary. The estuary is crossed by Barmouth Bridge, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> railway viaduct opened in 1867. In Welsh tradition the river marked a boundary of the early kingdom of Meirionnydd.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afon-mawddach.jpg" alt="Afon Mawddach" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Entopos · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mawddach_Estuary_from_above_Barmouth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Mawddach rises in a wide area north of Dduallt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a> and runs for 28 miles (45 km). It is much branched, and many of its tributaries are of a similar size to the main river: the Afon Cwm Mynach from the Rhinogydd, the Afon Gamlan, which falls through ancient oak woods by the Rhaeadr Ddu at Ganllwyd, the Afon Eden through the Coed-y-Brenin forest, the Afon Gain, the Afon Wen and the Afon Wnion from the east. The catchment is bounded by the Aran Fawddwy massif to the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a> dome to the west and north.</p><p>Below Dolgellau, where the Mawddach meets the Wnion, the river enters a wide, sandy estuary overlooked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadair-idris">Cadair Idris</a> (893 m) to the south and Diffwys (750 m) to the north. The estuary began as a glaciated valley flooded by the sea as the ice retreated, forming a fjord later filled with glacial deposits and, in the Holocene, gravel from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The estuary was a centre of shipbuilding in the 18th century and probably for some centuries before, though no trace of that work remains. The catchment has also seen gold mining and later gold panning, forestry, the preparation of animal skins, the storage of old munitions and the use of hillsides as artillery ranges, all of which left pollution behind. The Eden and the Gain were among the tributaries hardest hit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river rises and falls quickly with heavy rain on base-poor soils, which can sharply lower its pH, yet it supports a salmon and trout fishery. The old Great Western Railway branch line along the southern bank is now the Mawddach Trail, an 8-mile path for walkers and cyclists from Dolgellau to Morfa Mawddach, managed by the Eryri National Park Authority. The town of Barmouth, with its railway bridge, stands at the river's mouth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Afon Mawddach” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to Welsh genealogical tradition, Meirionnydd took its name from Meirion, a grandson of Cunedda, who was granted the land between the rivers <b>Mawddach</b> and Dyfi in the 5th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-meirionnydd">Kingdom of Meirionnydd</a></li></ul></section>
